# Output format

Emit `<introduction>` (optional — 2 to 5 sentences naming the rotation
axis and any ambiguities resolved), then `<requirements>`, whose
children are one or more `<responsibility>` blocks, each with exactly
one `<name>` and one `<feats>` block — nothing else.

# Rules

* `<name>` is a short noun phrase (2 to 8 words) naming one system-side
  concern. Good: "append-only event log", "per-request access
  decision". Bad: "User Authentication" (grouping — split it),
  "users can log in" (feature axis, not system-side), "authentication"
  (one word, vague).
* **One atom = one concern.** If the name has "and" in it, it's
  probably two atoms.
* `<feats>` is a flat list of zero or more `<feat id="..."/>` children
  naming every feature that implicates this atom, referencing feature
  names from the input verbatim. **Many-to-many is expected** — a
  login feature typically implicates session lifecycle, password
  hash, rate limit, and token refresh; tag it on all of them.
* **Empty `<feats/>` is legal** for system-emergent atoms with no
  direct feature cause — an append-only event log, a per-project
  sandbox root.
* **No two atoms share a name** (case- and whitespace-insensitive). If
  two candidates would collide, they're either the same atom or need
  sharper names.
* **Every feature in the input must appear in at least one atom's
  `<feats>`.** A feature with no atom tag is a rotation gap — if a
  feature looks like it has no system side, look again; every feature
  imposes some system-side obligation, even if only "persist this
  preference".
* **Break feature boundaries — that is the point.** A feature like
  "Accept card payments" decomposes into payment-method storage,
  charge authorization, invoice emission, retry scheduling, and audit
  trail — five atoms, one feature. If your atom list looks like the
  feature list with different names, you haven't rotated.
* **No atom-count ceiling — prefer splitting when uncertain.**
  Clustering is sysarch's job, not yours.
* **Atomize platform-NFR concerns too**, not just feature-derived
  work: rate limiting, audit logging, circuit breakers on external
  calls, retry with backoff, encryption at rest, credential rotation,
  quota tracking, license/compliance obligations. These typically have
  empty `<feats/>` since they govern existing features rather than
  covering new ones. Sysarch can only cluster what you emit — an NFR
  missing from this list is a missing policy candidate downstream. Do
  not try to distinguish local from cross-cutting here; that is an
  architecture-informed call sysarch makes.
* Output only `<introduction>` and `<requirements>` — no
  meta-commentary.
